const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Grant Writing Service","serviceType":"Research grant proposal writing, review, editing and budget justification","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/grant-writing/","description":"Proposals written and reviewed against the funder's own rubric — NIH, NSF, Horizon Europe, ERC, Wellcome, UKRI, ICMR. Eligibility checked first.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I structure a grant proposal to increase funding success?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Structure it around the funder’s criteria rather than around a generic outline, because the criteria are the order in which it will be read and scored. The sections almost every scheme wants are a title and summary, the background and the gap, a problem statement, objectives or specific aims, the methodology, a timeline, a budget with its justification, the expected outcomes and impact, and the supporting documents. What differs — and what decides the outcome — is the weight each one carries. A Horizon Europe proposal gives a third of its assessment to impact, so impact is a major section with its own structure. An ERC proposal has no impact criterion at all, and the same pages are better spent on the idea. An NIH Research Strategy has twelve pages for significance, innovation and approach, and nothing else scores. Write to the rubric, then check the structure against the funder’s template rather than against the last proposal you wrote."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the key components of a compelling grant application?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A problem a reviewer can verify is real, objectives that are specific and not contingent on one another, a methodology detailed enough that its feasibility can be judged, outcomes stated as things that will be true rather than things that will be attempted, a budget that visibly buys the workplan, a timeline with dependencies in it, and an explicit line connecting all of that to what this funder has said it wants. Two components carry more weight than applicants expect. The first is the alternative approach: naming what happens if the central method fails is what separates a proposal that reads as feasible from one that reads as hopeful. The second is the justification of the team — why these people, with this combination of methods, rather than any other group who could have proposed this."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do professional grant writing services help improve proposal quality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Mostly by reading it as a reviewer will rather than as its author does. A professional grant writing service brings three things you cannot easily supply yourself: familiarity with the rubric that will be applied, including what this funder does not assess; a reader who has never seen the project and therefore notices where the argument is assumed rather than made; and the discipline of ranking changes by what each is worth instead of by how satisfying it is to make. Alongside that sits the unglamorous half of the work — eligibility, page limits, formatting, annexes, CV formats, budget categories and portal fields — which ends more applications than weak science does and which nobody enjoys doing at eleven at night. What it does not do is improve the research. If the idea is not competitive for this scheme, a better document makes that clearer rather than less clear, and we would rather say so early."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What common mistakes should be avoided in grant proposals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Eight, in roughly the order we see them. Writing to the wrong funder, so the document answers criteria nobody is applying. Objectives that depend on one another, so a reviewer who doubts the first doubts all of them. A methodology written at the level of a published paper’s methods section, which is far too thin to judge feasibility from. A sample size stated as a number, with the assumptions behind it missing. Impact treated as a closing paragraph where it is a scored criterion with a threshold. A budget fitted to the ceiling rather than built from the work. Reviewer comments from a previous round left visibly unanswered. And leaving the annexes until last — letters of support, data management plans and ethics self-assessments are where applications fail on the final afternoon. Six of those eight are decided before any drafting starts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can expert guidance help align research goals with funding requirements?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By starting from what the funder has said it will buy and working back to your project, which is the opposite of how most applications are written. That means reading the call text and the work programme rather than the scheme’s homepage, looking at what this funder has recently paid for in your area, and being honest about the distance between your question and its priorities. Sometimes the alignment is real and simply has not been stated, and saying it plainly in the first paragraph is most of the work. Sometimes the alignment is partial, and the project can be reframed or the scope adjusted without distorting the science. And sometimes there is no alignment, in which case the useful guidance is which other scheme fits — a redirection that costs an hour and saves four months. Expert guidance is worth most at that first decision and least once drafting is under way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose between funders, and does it really change the document?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It changes it completely, which is why the same proposal sent to two funders usually loses at both. The published rubrics genuinely disagree. At the ERC, excellence is the sole criterion of evaluation and the first step reads only a five-page synopsis and a four-page CV — your methodology and your workplan are not in front of the panel yet. At Horizon Europe, impact is one of three equally weighted criteria, each with a threshold of 3 out of 5, so an outstanding proposal with a thin impact section is rejected on that alone. At NIH, the current framework scores the importance of the research and the rigour of the approach separately, and instructs reviewers that the importance score sets the limit on the best possible overall impact score. At ICMR, the whole rationale has to fit in five hundred words. Choose the funder first, from eligibility and genuine fit, then write one document properly for it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a proposal take to write properly?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a major research grant, plan on three months from a standing start and treat six weeks as the minimum that does not compromise the document. The drafting itself is a smaller part of that than people expect. The long poles are the things that depend on other people: partner commitments and letters of support, institutional costings and approvals, ethics pre-checks, and getting co-investigators to send CVs in the funder’s format. A review of an existing draft takes about a week, which is why it is worth commissioning three or four weeks out rather than three days out. If you have less time than this, say so at the first conversation — we will tell you honestly what can be done well in the time available, and occasionally that the honest answer is to aim at the next round instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you prepare the budget, and can you justify it to the funder’s rules?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, built from the workplan rather than fitted to the ceiling, and costed to the categories the scheme uses — the cost principles in the Uniform Guidance at 2 CFR Part 200 for United States federal awards, Horizon Europe’s actual and unit cost categories, full economic costing in the United Kingdom, and recurring and non-recurring heads for Indian national funders. The justification matters more than the arithmetic: a reviewer is asking whether the money buys the work, not whether the total is modest, and an under-costed proposal reads as naive rather than as good value. We work alongside your research office or finance team rather than around them, because the institutional rate agreements and approvals are theirs and the final figures have to be ones your institution will sign."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if the proposal is rejected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"First, read the critique as evidence rather than as a verdict, and give it a week before deciding anything. Most summary statements contain three kinds of comment: criticisms that are factually answerable, criticisms that point at a real weakness, and criticisms that reveal a reviewer misread something — which is usually a writing problem rather than a reviewer problem, and is the most fixable of the three. Then the question is whether to resubmit, redirect or stop. Resubmission rules differ: NIH allows one resubmission with an introduction answering the previous critique, and a C grade at the ERC carries a restriction on reapplying that a B does not. A resubmission that does not visibly answer the earlier comments tends to attract them again, so the introduction is worth as much care as any section. Sometimes the right answer is that the proposal was fine and the call was wrong, and it should go elsewhere largely unchanged."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will Pubrica be named on the application, and is this ghostwriting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and no. The application is submitted in your name by your institution, and the applicant is accountable for it — every funder requires that, and it is a condition rather than a formality. What we provide is drafting, review and editing support, which is ordinary in research and is used by a great many funded groups. Where a funder asks whether professional assistance was used, the answer is yes and we help you say so. What we will not do is invent preliminary data, describe a collaboration that has not been agreed, overstate what a pilot showed, or write claims you cannot stand behind at interview — and an interview is exactly where an overwritten proposal comes apart, because the panel asks about the sentence you did not write. Any use of AI in drafting is declared to you, and we follow whatever your funder’s policy says about it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Am I eligible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Worth establishing before anything else, because it is checkable in an afternoon and it is the failure that ends four months of work. Three of the rules moved during 2026: both ERC career windows widened for the 2027 calls, so the familiar two-to-seven and seven-to-twelve year figures are now historic; NIH restructured how international collaborations are funded; and Wellcome’s geographic eligibility narrows on 29 October 2026. You can run the mechanical part yourself in the free grant eligibility checker, which prints the published rule beside its answer for eight schemes. We check it in the first conversation, before any writing is quoted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How far ahead should I start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a single-investigator proposal, eight to twelve weeks before the deadline is comfortable and four is tight but survivable. For a consortium, four to six months, because the constraint is never the writing — it is assembling letters of support, agreeing the budget across partners, and getting institutional sign-off from organisations with their own internal deadlines. What actually goes wrong at the end is rarely the science: it is a partner who has not returned their section, a finance office that needs ten working days, and a portal that closes at a time zone nobody checked. Those are all solvable at week ten and not at day three."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you write the data management plan and the impact section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and they are worth more attention than they usually get. A data management plan is assessed against the funder’s own policy and against FAIR principles, and a generic one is visibly generic; it has to name the data types, the repositories, the metadata standard, the retention period and who is responsible. Impact under Horizon Europe is a third of the assessment and a hard gate, and it is scored on pathway and measures rather than on ambition. Both are rewritten per funder rather than carried across, because a section written for one framework frequently earns nothing at all under another."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you write for industry, charity and national funders as well?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Applications have been written or reviewed for twenty-seven funders across the United States, Europe, the United Kingdom and India, including the major national agencies, the European frameworks, the large medical research charities, and the SBIR and STTR programmes. The differences between them are not cosmetic. A charity panel that includes patient reviewers reads a lay summary as a scored section rather than a formality. An SBIR proposal is assessed on commercial potential as well as science. A national agency in India may ask for the health-outcome case in five hundred words. We write to the rubric that will be used, which means the document is different each time."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is a resubmission different from a first submission?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is a different document with a different reader, and treating it as a revised first submission is why resubmissions fail twice. The panel has the previous summary statement, and the introduction to the resubmission is where the application is won or lost: it has to acknowledge each substantive criticism, say what changed, and — where you disagree — say so with evidence rather than absorbing the point silently. Reviewers notice both evasion and capitulation. It also matters that the science moved: a resubmission with new preliminary data addressing the central concern is a far stronger case than the same proposal written more carefully."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Grant writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/grant-writing/"}]}];

